From caf-talk Caf Apr 18 00:26:47 1994
From: me95004@black.ox.ac.uk (Malcolm Hutty)
Subject:  Birmingham University busted
Message-ID: <1994Apr17.220925.29351@black.ox.ac.uk>
Date: Sun, 17 Apr 1994 22:09:25 GMT

I saw on the TV news that Birmingham University (UK) has been busted by the 
police for "having child pornography stored on its computers". There was 
apparently a tip-off "from a source in America". Does anyone have any 
concrete facts about what this means?

Nothing I say should be taken to indicate that I condone child porn in 
any way. That said, it is clear that "child porn" is the latest menace that 
everyone from PC Plod and the local social to John Gummer use to threaten 
everyone into giving up their most basic civil liberties. Let us consider 
what this news could mean:

o Perhaps someone at Birmingham knowingly held a GIF store of this 
  material. Suggested solution: lock 'em up.

o This could simply mean that Birmingham Uni has a USENET feed. Surprise, 
  surprise. Any university with a Usenet feed is vulnerable, as anyone can 
  post anything to any newsgroup. We have to face it, we cannot have USENET 
  and a completely effective ban on child porn.

o Even less than a news feed, anyone with telnet has access to computers 
around the world that do have Usenet feeds, and will grant anonymous 
access. Presumably there are anonymous and non-anonymous ftp sites 
holding child porn around the world. It is quite possible that the police 
failed to make a distinction between material stored at Birmingham, and 
material accessible from Birmingham.

o At a minimum, having access to email allows the possibility that you 
  can receive text and UUENCODEd GIF images, perhaps even unsolicited. 
  There have been cases of legal porn retailers in the USA receiving 
  UNSOLICITED child porn FROM THE POLICE IN A STING OPERATION, and then 
  being busted LEADING TO CONVICTION even though the retailer hadn't even 
  opened the envelope and did not know the contents.
  I'm not saying that that happened in this instance, but it does remind 
  us that just because someone shouted "child porn" doesn't mean we should 
  lose our common sense.

Just think: anyone reading this, EVEN YOU, could be CONVICTED of 
possessing child porn, all it would take was for me to write some obscene 
comments about children in this very article. You would never know until 
you read it. It makes you realise how vulnerable we are to the 
forces of authority that are deliberately marshalling public horror at 
child porn in order to justify vigourous regulation of the most free 
country in the world, our country, the Internet.
-- 
