Message-ID: <2ot27v$i19@senator-bedfellow.MIT.EDU>

In article <RAOUL.94Apr17142826@m4-035-11.mit.edu> raoul@athena.mit.edu (Nico Garcia) writes:
>In article <2om65a$9fr@mojo.eng.umd.edu> russotto@eng.umd.edu (Matthew T. Russotto) writes:

>   At UMCP, lots of people used
>   to use the printers, workstations, etc, to print out newsletters-- they
>   may still, for all I know.  The reason was to save a buck (at the
>   University's expense), not because the newsletters themselves were
>   illegal.

>We're not specifically talking about a newsletter on a laser printer:
>in fact, as long as you don't go over your personal quota, nobody
>minds if one does that at MIT, either.

Even after the (relatively recent) introduction of print quotas, printing
large numbers of copies of a single document is against MIT policy.  In
general MIT doesn't care what you print in small quantities, but the
pervasive anti-harassment rules presumably apply to printers as well and
prohibit their use for printing certain types of documents.

However, I would say that there is at best a moderate correlation between
violations of the rules of use of the MIT system, interpreted strictly,
and actual harm (losses of data, violations of privacy, legitimate cases
of harassment, etc.), and criminal misuse has been very rare despite the
large number of violations of MIT's internal rules.  Probably the most
common crime related to the computer system is people stealing parts of it.


--
    John Carr (jfc@mit.edu)

