In article <2ou6mj$7gm@mojo.eng.umd.edu> russotto@eng.umd.edu (Matthew T. Russotto) writes:

   It could be.  But my point is that deliberate misuse of univerity
   property is not prima facia proof of intent to commit a crime, as the argument
   I was responding to was clearly asserting.  

A valid point. The extent of his actions, his concealment of them,
their unusual nature, and the fact that resources existed for
legitimate publication or storage of publicly useful files (such as
the rtfm.mit.edu ftp server, private email, a reasonable request for
additional disk space, or buying and installing his own machine on the
MIT net) all very strongly indicate an awareness, even an intent, of
criminal use of the BBS. I have still seen no claims that there was
*any* legitimate use of it, for non-copyrighted files. Does anyone
know if there was?

In and of itself, of course, violating a university rule is hardly
proof of intent to commit a crime, agreed. It is in this case very
strong circumstantial evidence.

It's also proof that people who "hack systems" can and do commit crimes
in the process. MIT's security has been pretty user friendly, I think
with the idea that if a student really wants to poke around, they can
learn something in the process. This kind of case shows that that
trust is sometimes profoundly abused. Ergo, for a safe system, additional
security is necessary, even if it is a pain in the ass or makes it
harder to learn systems. Shame, really.

