In article <1994Apr19.172720.10014@msus1.msus.edu> manohar@msus1.msus.edu writes:
>By the way I think it's really *riduculous* to refer to someone's referrin
>to Kathiravan a a 'chimp' as a racial slur. If you visit TN, you'll
>see several people arguing (eg. women near water pumps) with 'Naye',
>'Kurange' and a lot of other expressions, but these people could even be
>from the same family, and as far as I know there are no dog or monkey races
>in TN, who argue in Tamil.

This is very interesting indeed.  The form that cussing
takes in a culture is probably worth more attention
than is usually given.

There seem to be four kinds of cusswords:
  1. denial of human status;
  2. implying verboten sexual conduct;
  3. religious blasphemy; and
  4. reference to consumption or immersion in waste
products.
  (There are probably more.)

The "naaye, kurange" that issues so effortlessly like a
jet of water from certainly very chaste Tamils whenever
they are in the vicinity of a faucet or hydrant
obviously belongs to category 1.  3 doesn't seem to
figure at all in Tamil culture.  On the other hand, 2
takes pride of place.

I don't know where I saw this, but the choice of
cussword is intimately bound to what the culture holds
dear.  (If anybody recognizes this reference, please do
share.)  Since Tamil culture enforces a view of mothers
and sisters as objects (sic) of inhuman iconic power,
the implying of a sex act with them is simply a
photographic negative of the iconic (picture-taking)
process --- it is simply another form of faithfulness
to the cultural ideal.  (Another example: A common
North American expletive is "shit", which tallies well
with the high priority that N. Americans place on
cleanliness.  Tamil analogues of "shit" qua cussword
are curiously absent.  The writer Sujatha once tried to
introduce "malam" as a cussword in one of his
futuristic stories, but I doubt it has made any headway
at all in its proverbial journey toward the Tamil fan.)

Of course, information is inevitably lost in this
process.  Real moms get mixed up with iconic mothers
and other icons; people who are obvious orphans get
called motherfuckers routinely by Tamil speakers; and
even when a net.Tamilatva like Selvajji is spoiling for
a fight, one of the trusted arrows in his quiver is to
casually frame his opponent as having made "oblique,
nasty remarks about my mother" :-> :-> :->, and then
hope that all-round consensus on anti-anti-mother
outrage will drown out the need for proof.

Finally, the original can certainly be different from
what we get when we make a picture out of the
photographic negative.  Thus, the occasional use of
"motherfucker" among North Americans has a different
component than its Tamil variant --- the N.A. cuss is
an abstract, broad-brushed attempt to expose a mama's
boy (tm), and/or to imply the most graphic violation of
the taboo against sex with close relatives.

In any case, the prevalence of the mother figure as sex
object in Tamil culture is an interesting fact, and
probably capable of supporting an infinite braid of
interpretations and reinterpretations.  I'd be grateful
to hear from my sct colleagues who have more
understanding in the matter.

Thanks for the conversation.

