From caf-talk Caf Apr 23 16:34:19 1994
Newsgroups: soc.culture.tamil,alt.comp.acad-freedom.talk
From: kadie@cs.uiuc.edu (Carl M Kadie)
Subject: Re: SCT, Freedom of Expression and Garbage.
Message-ID: <CoqBDL.JKy@cs.uiuc.edu>
Date: Sat, 23 Apr 1994 20:24:56 GMT

MFPD@utcvm.utc.edu writes:

[...]
>The words on the T-shirt you are referring to was clearly political
>speech.  The posting in question was neither political nor of any
>social value.  I am told that the courts have made a distinction
>between political and other forms of speech.
[...]

The distinction may not be as sharply drawn as you think:

From the ACLU Briefing Paper on Artistic Freedom (see refs):

   'Some legal scholars have argued that the First Amendment is only
   applicable to written or spoken _political_ expression, but the
   U.S.  Supreme Court has long rejected that interpretation.  In a
   1948 decision, the Court stated "We reject the suggestion that the
   constitutional protection for free speech applies only to the
   exposition of ideas.  The line between the informing and the
   entertaining is too elusive...."'

[...]
>In any case, the point I was trying to make was that the right of
>freedom of expression is not a blanket right.  Even governments,
>whose powers are restricted by the bill of rights, are allowed to
>regulate free speech.
[...]

Yes, but the fact that they can regulate *some* speech does not imply
that they can regulate *all* speech or *this* speech.

>Therefore it is not unreasonable to want to have some minimum decency
>standard for posting in SCT.

I don't see how this follows. I see no connection between the fact
that the U.S. government can regulate *some* speech (e.g. threats
against the President) and the reasonableness your wish for a decency
standard.

>That cannot be construed as an infringement of freedom of expression.

I think the Supreme Court would disagree.

   In Papish v. University of Missouri, 410 U.S.  667, 93 S.Ct. 1197,
   35 L.Ed.2d 618 (1973), the Supreme Court ordered the reinstatement
   of a university student expelled for distributing an underground
   newspaper sporting the headline "Motherfucker acquitted" on the
   grounds that "the mere dissemination of ideas--no matter how
   offensive to good taste--on a state university campus may not be
   shut off in the name alone of conventions of decency."  Id. at 670,
   93 S.Ct. at 1199.
      [An excerpt from _Doe v. University of Michigan_, 721 F. Supp. 852
      (E.D. Mich. 1989)]

>"Community standard" for matters of this sort is a doctrine accepted
>by the supreme court.

"Community standard" is not accepted by the Supreme Court for matters
of this sort (vulgar words). It is only accepted in the context of
allowing some (but not all) pornography and erotica to be banned.

>However, I understand that there is no administrative
>mechanism in the net to do anything about it.

Agreed.

>But speaking out against such repugnant posting cannot be equated
>with abridging freedom of expression.

Agreed, there is nothing wrong with speaking out against that which
you find repugnant. Indeed in some case, I think it is your duty.

I do, however, think there is something wrong with speaking out
against someone's right to express that which you find repugnant.

Put another way, I think it is better to criticize the content of
someone's speech than it is to criticize that person's right to say
it.  Voltaire, 1694-1778, but it even more strongly:

        "I disapprove of what you say, but I will defend
         to the death your right to say it."

- Carl


