                              NEW THINKING
                              January 1994
 
 
PROVOCATION: SPECIAL U.N. CURRENCY
The U.S. reluctance to pay its contribution suggests that the
U.N. should print up to $5 billion (U.S.) a year of its own
currency.  All U.N. member states would agree to accept this
currency at a U.S. dollar equivalent value.  The value could also
be set against a basket of currencies (dollar, yen and mark).
The global inflationary effect of this increase in money supply
would be totally insignificant (and even beneficial).  The U.N.
would be freed from the delays and political pressures of
contributions from member states.  Member states would welcome
this release.
 
CONCEPT:  INTERNATIONAL SUPREME COURT
For a country such as South Africa, there would be great value in
an International Supreme Court that would protect the
constitution and the rights of citizens.  An International
Supreme Court would not be subject to local political pressures
or to political stacking.  There could be one such court or
several.  The court would function much as the Hague Court does
but would be concerned with internal matters.  The British Empire
used to have the Privy Council, which had a similar international
role.
 
DIRECTION:  BETTER THAN DEBATE
Our much-esteemed debate system is remarkably ineffective for
constructive thinking.  The clash of views was designed to remove
untruth, not to develop new options.  At the very best, there
might be a synthesis of opposing views, but even this provides
only one further option.  Instead of debate, we need the
constructive, creative and design energy of parallel thinking in
which all parties think constructively in parallel to design a
way forward.  The most practical way of carrying out parallel
thinking is to use the Six Hat method, which is now being widely
used by major corporations (IBM, Du Pont, Prudential, etc.)
because it provides a powerful constructive framework that
eliminates the personal politics of debate.
 
PROVOCATION:  U.N. TO EXPEL U.S.
If the U.S. were to leave the U.N., then the U.N. would collapse
and disappear.  But if the U.N. were to expel the U.S., then both
would be strengthened.  The U.N. could become the other
superpower.  The U.S. would be free to develop its own foreign
policy.  If both parties agreed on a policy or action, then they
could visibly cooperate.  If they disagreed, then both would be
relieved of the responsibility of doing anything at all.  As it
is, the U.S. dominates the U.N. but is frustrated by the U.N.
control of U.S. policy.  At the same time, the U.N. is so
dependent on the U.S. that the U.N. cannot develop a truly
independent policy.
 
PREDICTION:  THE FAILURE OF TRADITIONAL THINKING
Traditional thinking is becoming increasingly inadequate in a
changing world.  Traditional thinking is based on definitions and
judgment.  This sort of thinking is fine for a stable world in
which labeled situations lead to appropriate action.  But this
traditional thinking is proving very inadequate in a rapidly
changing world because the flora and fauna of concepts and
situations are not what they used to be.  Judgment needs to be
replaced by design.  This demands new thinking for which
traditional thinkers have been ill-equipped by education and
experience.
 
SUGGESTION:  PUBLIC RELATIONS PERSPECTIVE PROBLEM
Why do most people believe that the U.N. is expensive when it is
an extraordinary bargain?  The pizza business and the legal
business in the U.S. are both $35 billion industries.  The soft
drink business accounts for $45 billion.  IBM has sales of $64
billion.  The regular U.N. budget of about $2.46 billion
(1992/93) is only just over 1% of the U.S. defense budget ($239
billion for 1993) and about the size of the toilet-tissue market.
A full-page advertisement in major newspapers could put things
into perspective.  But this would be seen as a misuse of U.N.
funds.  There needs to be a separately funded body that could say
such things on behalf of the U.N.
 
PREDICTION:  STRONG REGIONAL U.N. COUNCILS IN CHARGE
When the international community has a potato that is too hot to
handle, it unloads it on the U.N. The U.N. will become
increasingly skilled at passing the hot potato to regional
bodies, which will be too weak to handle matters.  The U.N. will
endeavor to set up strong regional councils within the U.N. that
will have first-line responsibilities for their regions.  The
Security Council will welcome this unloading of responsibility.
 
NOTE:  THE INTELLIGENCE TRAP
Are people with a high intelligence good thinkers?  This belief
is particularly held by those who have a high intelligence.  Yet
many people with a high intelligence get caught in the
intelligence trap and become poor thinkers.  A highly intelligent
person can take a view on a subject and use that high
intelligence to defend that view.  The defense will be so good
that the thinker sees no need to consider alternatives or to
listen to others.  As a result, many excellent minds are trapped
in poor ideas because these ideas are so well defended.
 
PROVOCATION:  DIFFERENT BERET COLORS IN ACTION
Perhaps U.N. troops should wear red berets when on a protection
mission to distinguish them from the purely humanitarian
operation.  Or humanitarian missions should switch to a new white
beret.  The clarification of missions would help to clarify
thinking about the matter and on-the-spot tactics.  Confusion can
arise when an apparent humanitarian mission has to assume a
fighting role--as in Somalia.
 
PROVOCATION:  SELLING THE MEDIA RIGHTS TO CONFLICTS
Should television companies compete for and buy the media rights
to each conflict area?  Then CNN might have to buy the media
rights to Bosnia or Somalia.  The Olympic Games exist today only
because Peter Ueberroth started an auction for TV rights that now
contributes in excess of $400 million for each Olympics.  Where
would the money go?  To a fund for those killed or injured in
action on U.N. business, and for refugees.  Is news a profitable
business?  Possibly not, but if it is, then perhaps the
performers should benefit.
 
SUGGESTION:  NO CONTACT NEGOTIATION
There is a useful, but little used, negotiating procedure in
which the parties never meet and in which there is no to and fro
at all.  Each party designs the most reasonable outcome.  A judge
or a panel chooses the most reasonable.  Since both parties must
design toward the middle to have any chance of success, it does
not really matter which suggestion is chosen.  This excellent
method is little used because lawyers prefer to start at the
extremes and fight toward the center rather than get there by
design.
 
THINK ABOUT:  THE POWER OF IMAGINED POSSIBILITIES
Contrary to what many believe, the excellence of western science
and technology has little to do with the argument system set up
by the Greek gang of three (Socrates, Plato and Aristotle).
Western science and technology have been driven by the
possibility system.  The possibility system provides the
hypothesis framework through which science looks at data.  The
possibility system also provides the vision toward which
technologists work.  Progress comes about through better
hypotheses and newer visions--not through criticism of the old
ones.
 
SUGGESTION:  FOCUSED CREATIVE COMMISSIONS
The U.N. should set up a framework for organizing Creative
Commissions on selected issues.  For example, there might be such
commissions on sovereignty or on sanctions.  The specific purpose
of these commissions would be to put forward new concepts.
Instead of the usual reference to p2ecedent and the past, the new
concepts would allow reference to new possibilities such as Dual
Sovereignty.  The generation of such possibility frameworks will
permit new thinking in difficult situations.  We do not always
have to work within the concepts of the past.
 
NOTE:  NEW THINKING CONTRIBUTION FORMAT
NEW THINKING is a platform for new thinking.  Contributions
should take the form of Suggestions, Provocations, Predictions,
Concepts, Directions and Think About items.  Each item must be
fewer than 151 words.  Items are published without a name
attached.  This is because new ideas must stand on their own and
to allow contributions from any source.  Any item will be
published only if it offers new thinking.  There is insufficient
space for comment-type correspondence.
 
PROVOCATION:  POLL VICTIMS
In a democracy, mass thinking has never had the legitimacy of
mass feeling.  Mass thinking is largely driven by media thinking,
which has to prefer selective interest over perspective truth.
Maybe the progress of civilization has been halted by a technical
communication excellence that reverses representative democracy.
Politicians are now the victims of instant polls that are
themselves the victims of media selectivity.
 
CONCEPT:  PIEBALD GUYS
Difficult situations are sustained by day-to-day continuity
simply because there is no way out for those involved.  If the
continuity is broken, then the situation may be resolved.
Leaders are often locked into a position and have to go forward
because there is no other way to go.  The designing and crafting
of ways out is an area of key importance that needs a better word
than bribe.  There can be windows of amnesty limited in time or
targeted at specific ranks (number two or three in the
hierarchy).  Simplistic notions of crime and punishment are not
always the best basis for action when both the good guys and the
bad guys are pretty piebald.
 
CONCEPT:  PERMANENT CONFLICT COUNCILS
The traditional withdrawal of ambassadors is a useful step along
the scale of hostility but a functional absurdity.  In any
conflict situation, the U.N. should seek to have a team attached
to both sides so the expression of views is always clear and
direct.  Instead of ad hoc contracts and negotiations, there
should be a permanent conflict council made up of both sides.
This would not in any way be a negotiating council but a
communication one.  Otherwise communications tend to be conducted
via CNN, which is both marvelous and ridiculous.
 
SUGGESTION:  CHARGE INTEREST
The U.N. could charge 12% interest on unpaid contributions from
the first date when payment is due.  In times of tight budgets,
it now becomes difficult for governments to justify paying this
unnecessary expense.  This has been suggested often enough.  A
formal resolution to this effect would probably be resisted by
some of the habitual late payers.  A voluntary pledge would
visibly divide countries into first-rank U.N. members and the
humiliation of second-rank status.
 
SUGGESTION:  CELEBRATING FORWARD NOT BACKWARD
Self-congratulatory fireworks celebrations for the U.N.'s 50th
anniversary may be well deserved but will not do much to position
the U.N. for the future.  Unless there is some evidence of new
ideas, the celebrations may seal a successful past.  The U.N.
should use the occasion to demonstrate new thinking for the
future.  This could be done by a State of the World (Concept
Review) conference that would show the dying concepts, the
emerging concepts, the competing concepts and the new concept
needs in key fields such as health, ecology, trade, etc.  The
U.N. is given the opportunity to show intellectual leadership
that is more than a "patching up" function.
 
NOTE:  LOGICAL IN HINDSIGHT
All valuable creative ideas must always be logical in hindsight.
Otherwise, we would not be able to recognize their value.  An ant
on the trunk of a tree has a 1 in 8,000 chance of getting to a
specific leaf.  An ant on that leaf has a 100 percent chance of
getting to the trunk of the tree.  Once an idea has been
conceived, it will seem logical and obvious in hindsight.  But
getting to the idea in the first place is another matter.
 
THINK ABOUT IT:  TRAPPED BY WORDS WE USE
Words and concepts drive civilizations forward and then restrict
further change.  Words are encyclopedias of ignorance.  A word
freezes into permanence the thinking and culture that were in
existence at the time and which may now impede further progress.
Words carry a baggage of meanings and associations that force
upon us an obsolete way of looking at the world.  We may need to
develop an entirely new concept language, which may even be
visual.  We need ways of defining the new concepts that we must
design.  Without new words, we are sucked back into old thinking.
 
PROVOCATION:  IS DEMOCRACY SUITABLE FOR EVERYONE?
Why does everyone have to have democracy thrust at them?  This is
a weak system with two major defects.  Firstly, it does not
contain any mechanism for its own improvement--since those
benefitting from the current system reject any attempt to change
it.  Secondly, there is a need for background habits and history
which may not be present.  Is there room for Singapore style
"paternalism"?  An International Supreme Court could protect the
rights of citizens within an off-the-shelf constitution.
 
NOTE:  AN OFFICE OF CREATIVITY
The International Creativity Office has been set up
..to provide a direct focus on serious creative and design
thinking.
..to provide an organizing framework for creative thinking
sessions and commissions.
..to provide instruction and training in creativity and lateral
thinking.
..to provide a platform for new thinking.
The Creativity office is at the service of the international
community wherever there is perceived to be a need for new
thinking and new ideas.  New thinking is not a substitute for
existing thinking but a supplement.  When current thinking has
nothing further to offer, then it is logical to try creative
thinking.
 
SUGGESTION:  50TH CELEBRATION PHYSICAL SALUTE
For its 50th anniversary celebration, the U.N. should create a
visible international symbol.  This could take the form of a
greeting gesture or salute.  The right hand is to be held
upright, parallel to the face and at the same height.  All the
fingers are spread apart.  The fingers are then brought together
and then spread again.  This dynamic salute symbolizes separate
nations coming together for a joint purpose but still retaining
their individuality.
 
DIRECTION:  THE U.N. PERMANENT EMERGENCY FORCE
The U.N. will some day have its own emergency army of paid
volunteers.  An independent body that took out full-page
advertisements for recruits to this force would be swamped by the
response.  This testing of the water would be a powerful step to
making the idea happen.  Member countries would agree to permit
their citizens to serve such a force and agree to lend from their
own defense forces.  Alternatively, each nation's defense force
would have a volunteer unit ready to be transferred at a moment's
notice to the U.N. command.  The increasing political liability
of body bags returning to any country makes such a volunteer
approach inevitable.
 
PREDICTION:  FUTURE THINKING
Creative thinking will become more and more important in all
fields:
..because experience, information, analysis, judgement, debate
and political consensus will not be able to provide the new
thinking that is needed in a rapidly changing world.
..because we now know that in any self-organizing information
system like the human brain there is an absolute, mathematical
need for creativity and for provocation.  Otherwise we remain
trapped within concepts and perceptions determined by a
particular sequence of experience.
..because not all problems can be solved by finding and removing
the cause (or bad guys).  There may be a need to design a way
forward, leaving the cause in place.
..because the emphasis in our traditional thinking habits is on
analysis, definitions, labels and judgment which are adequate for
assessing the past but not for designing the future.
..because ad hoc reactive responses are not always enough.
 
SUGGESTION:  SHADOW CABINET
The authoritarian decisions of the Security Council are usually
necessary.  But there is a place for a shadow cabinet to work in
parallel with the Security Council and to publish its own view.
Sometimes the views of the two bodies would coincide and
sometimes they would differ.  At the very least, this arrangement
would force the Security Council to make its decisions very
carefully.  Such a shadow cabinet could be set up at any time by
a group of countries with the initiative to set it up.  There is
no need for a legal basis within the U.N.  If it worked well,
then a legal basis might follow.
 
PROVOCATION:  TAX VIOLENCE
Violence on television and in films encourages violence in
society at least amongst those in vulnerable environments.
Banning violence would upset free speech advocates, but the
violence could be taxed:  $2,000 per killing, $5,000 per murder
each time the show is shown.  The money goes to a fund for
victims of violence.  This way violence becomes a production cost
just like filming in Tahiti.  Producers now need to consider how
much expensive violence is strictly necessary for dramatic
effect.  Gratuitous violence is no longer so profitable.
 
THINK ABOUT:  PRINCIPLES AND PRAGMATISM
Law and thinking like to work with absolute principles.  The U.N.
cannot do so but must be seen to do so.  Hence the problems.  The
U.S. can lean on a small Haiti but not on a bigger country with
the same sort of regime.  The Gulf war was driven by oil
pragmatism under the guise of repelling aggression.  But
repelling aggression elsewhere does not have that pragmatism.
Perhaps the U.N. should no longer pretend to work by principles
but openly embrace pragmatism.
 
NOTE:  FORMAL METHODS FOR PRODUCING CREATIVITY
Creativity is no longer a matter of messing around in
brainstorming sessions and hoping an idea will emerge.  Serious
creativity is a way of thinking that can be learned, practiced
and applied to any situation.  There are deliberate and formal
methods that can be applied systematically.  Lateral thinking
means moving across patterns rather than going along with them.
Lateral thinking is for changing concepts and perceptions instead
of working harder with the same old concepts and perceptions.
You cannot dig a hole in a different place by digging the same
hole deeper.
 
PROVOCATION:  THE LENS-CAP COMMANDO APPROACH
The most effective use of U.N. peace-keeping funds would be to
equip a lens-cap commando of about 100 people.  This commando
would be dispatched at once to any new trouble spot.  The task of
the commando would be to make it impossible for TV to transmit
from that area (keeping the lens caps on).  This would relieve
the U.N. of any need to do anything about that area.  Everyone
knows that if an area is not on TV, then there is no pressure for
the U.N. to do anything (see the various trouble spots around the
world).

